===persian===

دههٔ شصت را...
در مهتاب شمردم...
دوازده بهارِ کوتاه بود....
در یک زمستانِ بلند...
در آفتاب شمردم...
سیزده زمستانِ کوتاه بود....
در یک بهارِ بلند...
اگر ریشه در خاک داری می‌رویی...
چه باور کنی چه انکار...
برگ‌ها و شاخه‌ها را سرما و گرما می‌سوزاند...
اما ریشه‌ها را نه....
می‌پرسی دههٔ شصت چگونه گذشت؟
این ضرب‌المثل را هیچ کجا نشنیده‌ام...
«تفنگت را که گرفتند...
تیر و کمان کودکی‌ات...
خطا نمی‌کند...»
بهمن ۱۳۶۹ در پاسخ به مجلهٔ گردون

===machine===

The Sixties...
I counted them in the moonlight...
they were twelve short springs....
inside one long winter...
I counted them in the sunlight...
they were thirteen short winters....
inside one long spring...
If your root is in the soil, you grow...
whether you believe it or deny it...
Cold and heat burn the leaves and the branches...
but not the roots....
You ask how the Sixties passed?
I have never heard this proverb anywhere...
"Once they have taken your rifle...
the bow and arrow of your childhood...
does not miss...."
Bahman 1369, in reply to Gardoon magazine

===lantern===

The Sixties —
I counted them by moonlight...
twelve short springs
inside one long winter...
I counted them by sunlight...
thirteen short winters
inside one long spring...
If your root is in the soil, you grow,
whether you believe it or deny it...
Cold and heat burn the leaves and the branches
but they do not burn the roots....
You ask me how the Sixties passed?
I have never heard this proverb anywhere —
"Once they have taken your rifle...
the bow and arrow of your childhood...
does not miss...."

Bahman 1369

===translation===

===footnotes===

دههٔ شصت is the Iranian ۱۳۶۰s — roughly 1981 to 1991 — which is to say the whole of the
war with Iraq and the three years after it. It is not the Gregorian sixties, and the poem is
unreadable if it is taken that way.

The date line names an occasion: *Bahman 1369, in reply to Gardoon magazine.* The poem is an answer
to a commission, which is why it opens by restating the question it was asked.

The last three lines are in quotation marks and introduced as a proverb the poet says he has never
heard anywhere — his way of coining one and disowning it in the same breath. The bow and arrow come
back on page 183, where an old man goes down the road looking for his childhood's.
